### Copying from External Images

You can copy from any image, not just prior build stages. Docker's `COPY --from=` accepts either a build-stage name (e.g., `builder`) or an external image reference (e.g., `nginx:latest` or `nginx@sha256:...`).

Examples:

```dockerfile
# copy a single file from the official nginx image
COPY --from=nginx:latest /etc/nginx/nginx.conf /nginx.conf

# copy a directory from a specific image digest (reproducible)
COPY --from=nginx:1.25.2@sha256:<digest> /usr/share/nginx/html /usr/share/nginx/html

# copy from a named build stage
FROM golang:1.21 AS builder
# ... build steps that produce /app/bin/myapp ...
FROM alpine:3.18
COPY --from=builder /app/bin/myapp /usr/local/bin/myapp
```

Notes and best practices:

- The external image must be accessible to the Docker daemon (it will be pulled from the registry at build time).
- Using image digests (`@sha256:...`) makes builds reproducible and avoids unexpected changes from image tags.
- For private registries, ensure build-time access (e.g., `docker login`, BuildKit secret/credential forwarding, or registry credentials configured for your CI).
- This feature is supported by modern Docker and BuildKit; older Docker engines may not support copying from arbitrary external images.
- Consider security and size implications: copying files from large or untrusted images can increase image size or introduce unexpected configuration. Inspect the source image and copy only the files you need.
